  "change": "Add fixture-backed Unicode normalization coverage without application-level filename normalization. Canonically equivalent NFC/NFD names resolve to one existing file on the macOS CI runner; Windows and Linux retain two distinct names. Exact UTF-8 BOM/CRLF content and revisions are checked on all three platforms.",
  "focused_tests": {
    "command": "cargo test --workspace --locked",
    "names": [
      "tests::unicode_normalization_matrix_preserves_bytes_across_filesystem_lookup"
    ],
    "result": "The focused Unicode normalization test and full workspace tests passed on Ubuntu, macOS, and Windows.",
    "jobs": {
      "ubuntu_job_id": 114145739895,
      "macos_job_id": 114146545775,
      "windows_job_id": 114146545665,
      "focused_test_job_ids": [
        114145739895,
        114146545775,
        114146545665
      ],
      "focused_test_log_line": "test tests::unicode_normalization_matrix_preserves_bytes_across_filesystem_lookup ... ok"
    }
  },
  "scenario_results": [
    "The SYNC-004 fixture now includes composed NFC and decomposed NFD path names, matching content strings, and exact BOM/CRLF bytes.",
    "On macOS, reads through either canonical path form return the original file bytes and revision; the vault snapshot contains one entry.",
    "On Windows and Linux, the two canonical path forms remain distinct entries, and each read and snapshot path retains the matching original content and spelling.",
    "The Rust path validator does not apply Unicode normalization; path lookup follows the host filesystem's behavior."
  ],
